    "youtubeId": "9C-yL1kBgH8",
    "title": "Physiotherapist: 3 Foods Secretly Fueling Your Chronic Pain",
    "promiseDescription": "Explains how chronic low-grade inflammation slows healing and increases pain sensitivity, and gives three practical dietary changes (fatty fish, colorful vegetables, olive oil) to help calm it.",
    "categoryId": "cervicogenic-neck",
    "duration": "Full Video",
    "publishedAt": "2024-07-24",
    "thumbnailUrl": "https://i2.ytimg.com/vi/9C-yL1kBgH8/hqdefault.jpg",
    "transcript": "If you got something that just won't heal, a tendon, a joint, a muscle or your back and you have done the physio, the stretching, stayed active, done everything right but still there is something there simmering. Have somebody asked you this: What are you eating? In this video I'm going to walk through the connection of the most people never hear. How the food is either feeding or cooling the inflammation that's slowing your recovery. There is a person sitting in my clinic who just cannot name what's wrong. I'm not ill they say but I'm not myself either and I recognize that instantly because I hear it every week. And maybe you recognize it too. You are not exactly ill but you are not either yourself. Mornings are hard to get going. Your body feels stiff. You're tired in a way that sleep doesn't fix it. Your mood sits lower than it used to be. Maybe the weight has crept up even though you don't think you are eating any more than before. And that nagging thing in the background. Your back, a joint, a tendon keeps simmering even though you are doing everything right. And you don't connect it to anything. You think it's probably just my age or I'm just tired. And here's the key part. You have gotten used to it. The feeling crept downhill so slowly that you no longer remember what good feels like. That is exactly how people describe once they have fixed it. One person said that had no idea how bad they felt until it lifted. Another woke up first time with no stiffness and couldn't believe the change. And neither did anything radical. Less sugar, more vegetables and bit more. A few weeks later energy was back up and their mood started lifting. The common thread in both: low-grade inflammation. Here's why I'm telling you this. Inflammation is just not as wailing in your ankle after the sprain. There's another kind. Chronic silent low grade. It doesn't necessarily hurt but it slows healing, raises pain sensitivity and makes the tissues more reactive. And your diet is one of the biggest levels. You can either fuel it or put it out. Think of it in this way. After the inflammation is like a fire alarm. You sprain your ankle. The alarm sounds. The fire crew shows up. Puts it out and leaves. Swelling, redness, pain and that's the repair process. Gone in a week and it's a good thing. Chronic inflammation is different. It's like a stove left on low in the background. No flames, no alarm but the smoke fills up the room so slowly that you stop noticing it. Your body gets used to it and the immune system idles on a low flame year after year. It doesn't show outside but it shows on the blood markers like CRP that they are elevated. And this silent inflammation is linked to chronic pain, slow healing and osteoarthritis. It doesn't show in the mirror but it shows in your healing process. But what causes it? This is where it gets really interesting. Think of food as a message. Every bite sends your body the answer to the same question: calm down or activate. What kind of stuff feeds the inflammation the most? Added sugar, especially sugary drinks. Trans fat in processed baked goods. Ultra-processed food and an excess amount of omega-6 fats. You don't need to remember this whole list. Just think of it this way: the more the factory has touched the food before it reaches your mouth, the more likely it speaks the language of inflammation. What cools the inflammation the best? Fatty fish, berries, dark leafy greens, olive oil, nuts and plenty of fiber. These aren't just superfoods. They are targeted anti-inflammatory foods found in real, minimally processed food. And here's where most people slip up: they try to chase everything at once. You don't need to. Three changes are enough and I will show you now how. 1. Fatty fish. At least twice a week. Salmon, mackerel or sardines. Why fish especially? Your body uses the omega-3s in fish as the raw materials to build inflammation's stand-down signals. The molecules that tell inflammation to calm down and go home. Without them, your body can start a fight, but it struggles to bring it to an end. EPA and DHA, in particular, lower inflammatory markers. If you don't eat fish twice a week, there's one thing worth hearing: This video is sponsored by Naturecan and I use their omega-3 myself. 1000 mg of EPA and DHA provides the key essential fatty acids needed at a clinical dose that actually matters. The products are also third-party lab tested, so the labels tell you the truth. Link and your discount code are down in the description. But food first, supplements second. 2. Add color to your plate, literally. Every color in vegetables represents different tools. Blueberry blue, spinach green, pepper orange. Each one acts on a different point in inflammatory pathways. So one color isn't enough. The more colors on your plate, the bigger the repair kit your body has to work with. They are not decoration, they are medicine without side effects. 3. Switch your main cooking fat to olive oil. If you are using sunflower oil or margarine, switch to extra virgin olive oil. It contains a compound called oleocanthal, which presses the same switch in your body that ibuprofen does. Same mechanism, but more gentle. One dose doesn't do much, but repeatedly, every single day, year after year, it always adds up. And now to this: Think back to yesterday. How many colors were on your plate? How many times did you eat? How many times did you search for something sweet? Keep those numbers in mind; most people are quite surprised. All three of these things you can do on your next grocery run, but there is one more thing to understand why this matters on your road to recovery. When your body is running on chronic low-grade inflammation, everything heals slower. Tissues are more reactive, your pain system is more sensitive, and recovery from exercise takes longer. Nutrition doesn't replace exercise or treatment, but they either work together or against each other. You can't exercise your way out of an inflammatory state if you are eating more of it every day. And to wrap up: Chronic inflammation is silent. It doesn't always hurt, but it slows down healing, and you might not notice until it's gone. Diet either fuels it or cools it. Three changes are enough to start: Fatty fish twice a week, color on your plate, and olive oil as your main fat. And remember, you are not broken. This is a biological state, and it's fixable. This doesn't replace individual care or a doctor's assessment; it complements them. I've put together a free guide on this. The link is down in the description. Leave a comment: which one of these three are you already doing, and which will be the one you start next? See you at the next one.",
